#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528023} :activity/id #uuid "6e55aeb2-db41-50e1-8b8c-a30ee7439c07" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528016 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e15c88f-073f-51b7-975c-10a55ae55f2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({y}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527487} {:answer/id #uuid "708792b5-dc3b-5780-9f52-2c74e75a789f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({y}^{2}-y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(y+{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527517} {:answer/id #uuid "a5326780-633d-5ab5-b3dc-89a0af570ddd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({y}^{2}+{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527647} {:answer/id #uuid "c6423a8b-a7b1-5897-84f4-54bf126d2b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({y}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{4}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527705} {:answer/id #uuid "e55803c6-1d95-5033-92aa-8a0607c42665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({y}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527759}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527759} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfc1653c-6513-5ab7-95f5-3af895b0238c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "33a96099-4464-57a6-87f9-11f8046b2b50" :question/math-academy-id "q-122387" :question/problem "![](images/b8/b8e332d7f53868313cc589e4ce5e991d2bcce88d15613111c4d0f920b678830d.png)\n\nWhich expression gives the total finite area enclosed between the curves $x={y}^{4}$ and $x={y}^{2},$ as shown above?\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(y)={y}^{4}$ and $g(y)={y}^{2}.$ First, we find the intersections of the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ {y}^{4} & ={y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{4}-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}({y}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}(y-1)(y+1) & =0 \\\\ y & =-1,0,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area we want consists of two regions: ${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}.$\n\n![](images/8b/8b4cbf07b9c2a6d949a58f39489889d2a3fdd4690664e530386f6f61d9aac5fd.png)\n\n- For the area ${A}_{1},$ we have $y\\in [-1,0],$ and the right function is $g(y)$ while the left function is $f(y).$ So, the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(g(y)-f(y))\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({y}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the area ${A}_{2},$ we have $y\\in [0,1],$ and the right function is $g(y)$ while the left function is $f(y).$ So, the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(g(y)-f(y))\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({y}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "240bde0a-edd2-5c94-9c50-9f441bdb08ef"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528018 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527958 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "058cac9d-5a5a-5ee7-9198-6ebfb7207e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({[5-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{2}{y}+2]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527319} {:answer/id #uuid "7023af54-631a-5dcf-bf27-5f2705dfb93d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({[3-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{2}{y}]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527516} {:answer/id #uuid "80f6d32e-5475-5dfc-9839-a9615e457aa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({[2+\\frac{2}{y}]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527559} {:answer/id #uuid "d10abc45-5b7c-5d5e-9462-2d886e2f6bc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({[5-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{2}{y}+2]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527730} {:answer/id #uuid "d30b7d3d-0dea-5470-83f6-553983d20016" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}({[5-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{2}{y}+2]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527733}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527730} :answer-field/id #uuid "24a6b34c-6b2b-59cf-97a5-55f1d3c4912c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7bcaaf90-d33e-5a2f-9583-78877f436c71" :question/math-academy-id "q-50330" :question/problem "![](images/31/31d4236bf58cd579c49c7259c32a2a9171e07c771b4c34040ac92896809f695f.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x=\\frac{2}{y}$ and $x=3-y$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=-2.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{y} & =3-y \\\\ 2 & =3y-{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-3y+2 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y-2) & =0 \\\\ y & =1,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=1$ to $y=2.$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/b7/b73c341f4d09b1df7bea162bc7dbe10d0ee60920c847f9c24210386a3d139e61.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=\\frac{2}{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=3-y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=3-y.$\n\n![](images/c8/c8c8e766c66e47a54f316f54893d603b245acec8f529da3a5033d2bba547a319.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\,(3-y)-(-2)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{[5-y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=\\frac{2}{y}.$\n\n![](images/1b/1b31331f1c0da353bee6fe29d10e9e58dd531cbc8152cbce214d1adea771b032.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{[\\,(\\frac{2}{y})-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{[\\frac{2}{y}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{[5-y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{[\\frac{2}{y}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({[5-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{2}{y}+2]}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2e35343d-8855-5067-afba-636871de1ae6"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528019 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188542 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ce17c65-7d67-54af-bb8e-a4b548957fdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500495} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc573f9-6cba-59f5-957c-6c9a34a77f1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500566} {:answer/id #uuid "62f66835-664a-578e-9ff4-452e7799954b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=r+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500673} {:answer/id #uuid "7e1c7097-61f8-5853-b475-d4c6e09f84d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=Kt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500728} {:answer/id #uuid "f60054c5-79dc-51b2-9d83-1ea1d762a52d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=Kr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500992}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500992} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e2c35a2-00a1-5395-9ca6-d665e10ade6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c8d1bea5-b961-5d22-9bfd-ba62f26db131" :question/math-academy-id "q-119161" :question/problem "The area of a circle increases at a rate that's proportional to the square of its radius. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** of a circle **increases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to the **square of its radius**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a circle* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate that's *proportional* to the *square of its radius.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=k{r}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe area of a circle is given by \n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=k{r}^{2} \\\\ 2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=k{r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k{r}^{2}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{kr}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{2\\pi }>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=Kr.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2edaff19-4811-510a-9c53-a513b91ee9dc"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528020 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234032 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "588c52dd-f244-5cf8-bb03-52cf34b6c535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2.343}{10}{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527475} {:answer/id #uuid "6859ef2c-19ed-5be4-a82c-9875ae292e49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{43}{100}{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527506} {:answer/id #uuid "6a31fdad-f025-5641-a528-bd1d0f98c5b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{43}{10}{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527509} {:answer/id #uuid "7dca9273-f96a-5ee0-a6d3-7e1206f51773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{43}{100}{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527545} {:answer/id #uuid "97432ddb-a0a4-5e0b-91fb-38bf9a401171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{43}{1\\,000}{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527611}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527509} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ebb4c90-61c1-5f3f-a84a-17bd726c40e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7b297bef-d86e-59df-8ce3-4a3826383e59" :question/math-academy-id "q-40691" :question/problem "$2.3\\overset{―}{43}=$" :question/worked-solution "The given repeating decimal can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2.3\\overset{―}{43} & =2.343\\,43... \\\\  & =2.3+0.043+0.000\\,43+0.000\\,004\\,3+… \\\\  & =2.3+{{\\frac{43}{1\\,000}+\\frac{43}{100\\,000}+\\frac{43}{10\\,000\\,000}+⋯.}_{⏟}}_{\\text{geometric series}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that we have a geometric series where the first term is ${a}_{1}=\\frac{43}{1\\,000}$ and the common ratio is $\\frac{1}{100}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2.3\\overset{―}{43} & =2.3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2.3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{43}{1\\,000}{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2.3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{43\\cdot 100}{1\\,000}{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n} \\\\  & =2.3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{43}{10}{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "32e5e4c0-83cd-51ce-a8af-0eb4cc07ace8"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528021 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527994 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07656df9-a2d3-55b7-968e-e7475835e5c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x-2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527321} {:answer/id #uuid "2710d395-2b8f-5a9c-8242-b8a14e7e70c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x-2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527396} {:answer/id #uuid "27f4d9d3-7e96-5c12-b5a3-f9372b87c8d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x-2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527398} {:answer/id #uuid "b4fa43a8-904c-5b53-a2e7-0be8f2877828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x-2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527676} {:answer/id #uuid "ca4bdf40-3643-5dc6-b993-f92752eaaf5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{3}-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x-2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527713}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527398} :answer-field/id #uuid "faa2ee71-4e17-514a-a333-0d36f72cde02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cef928b3-a5b9-5b14-83bc-17bf537996f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-96454" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{{tanh}^{2}⁡(3x-2)}{3}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the secant-tangent identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1-{tanh}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sech}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nSetting $\\theta =3x-2,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{tanh}^{2}⁡(3x-2) & ={sech}^{2}⁡(3x-2) \\\\ {tanh}^{2}⁡(3x-2) & =1-{sech}^{2}⁡(3x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{tanh}^{2}⁡(3x-2)}{3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int (1-{sech}^{2}⁡(3x-2))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int 1\\cdot \\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {sech}^{2}⁡(3x-2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x-2)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3e9453b3-3741-5c8a-8ead-49704c0ce73e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528023 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527939 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ab322b7-88c0-562d-aad6-28ae0e766ac0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4-x|-\\frac{5}{4-x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-x|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527327} {:answer/id #uuid "3b89cb41-52b7-5666-8f50-16d233ce418c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4-x|-\\frac{1}{4-x}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-x|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527427} {:answer/id #uuid "58923f50-9ab3-5f70-9ff3-7fb61c42ff32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4-x|-\\frac{5}{4-x}+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-x|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527476} {:answer/id #uuid "652ffd1d-a604-5671-a532-9fbbb91be568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4-x|+\\frac{1}{4-x}+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-x|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527499} {:answer/id #uuid "bf74c85d-c2cb-5a97-9f09-4ade326e74d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4-x|+\\frac{5}{4-x}-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-x|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527694}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527499} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1fdb08d-4e9a-5aa1-bf2f-9d236664e6fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "504684ef-507d-57b1-81c3-7c9672d3e28f" :question/math-academy-id "q-11372" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{9x-38}{(4-x)^{2}(2-x)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9x-38}{(4-x)^{2}(2-x)}=\\frac{A}{(4-x)}+\\frac{B}{(4-x)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(2-x)}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(4-x)^{2}(2-x)$ and get\n\n$$\n9x-38=A(4-x)(2-x)+B(2-x)+C(4-x)^{2}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $C$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(4)-38 & =A(4-4)(2-4)+B(2-4)+C(4-4)^{2} \\\\ 36-38 & =B(-2) \\\\ B & =\\frac{-2}{-2} \\\\ B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(2)-38 & =A(4-2)(2-2)+B(2-2)+C(4-2)^{2} \\\\ 18-38 & =C{(2)}^{2} \\\\ C & =\\frac{-20}{4} \\\\ C & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known values of $B$ and $C,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(0)-38 & =A(4-0)(2-0)+B(2-0)+C(4-0)^{2} \\\\ -38 & =8A+2B+16C \\\\ -38 & =8A+2(1)+16(-5) \\\\ -38 & =8A-78 \\\\ 8A & =78-38 \\\\ A & =\\frac{40}{8} \\\\ A & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{9x-38}{(4-x)^{2}(2-x)}=\\frac{5}{(4-x)}+\\frac{1}{(4-x)^{2}}-\\frac{5}{(2-x)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9x-38}{(4-x)^{2}(2-x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{5}{(4-x)}+\\frac{1}{(4-x)^{2}}-\\frac{5}{(2-x)})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\frac{1}{4-x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{(4-x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{2-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4-x|+\\frac{1}{4-x}+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-x|+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."} :step/id #uuid "53705ca3-b02d-5e95-925f-dae53d78647f"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528024 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527936 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d126f60-c244-55e8-99ea-c6908268f2e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527335} {:answer/id #uuid "1a86b6cb-7dc9-57c4-8aaa-4c6993228125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527364} {:answer/id #uuid "326e86ac-4b4d-58bd-bac0-9dc086c029d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527413} {:answer/id #uuid "bcc743be-6c0f-592e-bc22-3ef4125c7e4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527692} {:answer/id #uuid "e5fa9767-9f89-5829-885f-cdf43d1a2b7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527760}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527760} :answer-field/id #uuid "e62dccaf-73d1-5442-9e53-eebdcd332113" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "46e4a876-e7eb-598f-bbec-ff44b3be1391" :question/math-academy-id "q-118775" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x){|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)) \\\\  & =0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5804b103-5606-587e-abd2-0a03c2202262"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528026 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527995 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1432bb9a-ce4a-5f69-bb3d-d3b5455ac973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{3}({y}^{4}-4{y}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527350} {:answer/id #uuid "1ff669d2-de06-56d8-8f5c-68854882692a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}(y+2)\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527378} {:answer/id #uuid "73899ae8-32a9-5454-8013-2a30e4d6ada0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}({y}^{2}-2)\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527525} {:answer/id #uuid "a8cf2f80-e040-50b1-b53d-f4326f54cb9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{3}({y}^{2}-2)\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527654} {:answer/id #uuid "b00ae438-d1aa-56bc-a2ef-bf52d2a89e7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}({y}^{4}-4{y}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527669}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527669} :answer-field/id #uuid "315a9bb0-48e8-569d-b07b-4a5644ec8264" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d18dd5ed-e185-5cfc-926d-0d7b2a92c6df" :question/math-academy-id "q-120180" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $y=\\sqrt{x+2}$ and the $y$-axis from $y=2$ to $y=3.$ Which integral gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "The volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $x=g(y),$ the $y$-axis, and the horizontal lines $y=c$ and $y=d$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}[g(y)]^{2}\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write our equation in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{x+2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =x+2 \\\\ x & ={y}^{2}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in our example, $g(y)={y}^{2}-2$ and the limits of integration are from $y=2$ to $y=3.$\n\nFinally, the volume can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}{[g(y)]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}{({y}^{2}-2)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}({y}^{4}-4{y}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5b2f746e-59e2-5415-8c88-32df5e809be7"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528027 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47e00196-5dc7-5e37-af21-0f88f4308a05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527448} {:answer/id #uuid "64e1b1f8-db18-57e8-b34a-cb959e797fd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527498} {:answer/id #uuid "74015f70-4e31-59fa-a156-c84a3e6516a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527527} {:answer/id #uuid "91fc8920-5d08-5ae8-a052-34304d316f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527595} {:answer/id #uuid "a27a6cf4-0fa2-53b5-9118-7c658d9dc6a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527642}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527527} :answer-field/id #uuid "9aa35e99-bfe0-504f-b448-591e82ea534a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8222fa21-5fa3-52f1-a894-4b100aeb534d" :question/math-academy-id "q-10207" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d4bcdb6515b262c070c7ed689263efbbde8a350ad6a28b4fc106de281e69ff46.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2t,\\,y=t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a positive number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nTo solve the above integral, we use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & ⇒ & v=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =2t(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-2(\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -0\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)+2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =-2\\pi (-1) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5cd64603-ba44-5341-ad01-ca7767310751"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528028 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47ace125-1428-5370-906c-bbbbca66287f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "74" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527447} {:answer/id #uuid "7c5b7c2e-42ac-5c76-8d0e-167934a4ded1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527542} {:answer/id #uuid "83a8233c-86f1-5b7d-9702-f1ccf8ac79b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527562} {:answer/id #uuid "8a795631-2118-587a-9c52-e7fcae53626a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527580} {:answer/id #uuid "fbdf9a26-5fa5-5299-b0b5-3847f5e53bf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527794}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527580} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e7684e4-e06c-57a4-94c9-8c4065eb02cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "02b30695-cbca-5de7-a2d0-90b3f98db6af" :question/math-academy-id "q-75637" :question/problem "Consider the positive infinite geometric sequence that has a first term equal to $56$ and a third term equal to $\\frac{7}{8}.$ What is the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence?" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{1}=56$ and ${a}_{3}=\\frac{7}{8}.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{3-1} & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{8})}{56} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =(\\frac{7}{8})(\\frac{1}{56}) \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{64} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{64}} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we have taken the positive square root since we're told that the sequence is positive.\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{56}{(1-\\frac{1}{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{56}{(\\frac{7}{8})} \\\\  & =56(\\frac{8}{7}) \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5e7feaa4-b8d9-5448-8303-cd1422a89e2b"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528029 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351476 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5180e91d-b92b-505b-ba26-cfc2c02ae8d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-(2{e}^{2\\pi }+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349187} {:answer/id #uuid "59b0c393-ff53-5296-86d7-57e19a153481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2\\pi }+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349273} {:answer/id #uuid "8dfce7d1-3045-5f79-9f67-af6e541e7d40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{2\\pi }+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349724} {:answer/id #uuid "a1611af0-6b4b-511c-bdc0-8ccfe63b5718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{2\\pi }+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349880} {:answer/id #uuid "c6f0ee9b-6deb-5faa-8e4b-9fac96efdbec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{2\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350198}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349880} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2580160-8816-5519-b6e4-1d9a9bef341c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fbc072d0-2ec3-57b6-ba23-0219f96efe5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-83012" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(2{e}^{2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(2{e}^{2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2J \\\\ I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2(-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2I) \\\\ I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4I \\\\ 5I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2\\pi }}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ))-\\frac{{e}^{2(0)}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{e}^{2\\pi }}{5}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{2\\pi }+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "672645cd-294c-5987-920e-81f646ed0740"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528033 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528009 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d341268-ff40-5ed3-af0b-b6a741ea7f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{5\\,000}{1+4{e}^{-50t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527373} {:answer/id #uuid "45171aca-4721-5992-a53f-a77770463cf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{1\\,000}{1+49{e}^{-50t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527441} {:answer/id #uuid "62d90b2f-3cf4-56cb-8f20-42532d4a4769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{1\\,000}{1+4{e}^{-500t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527494} {:answer/id #uuid "6545848f-d1c2-504e-8ca0-db1a6d9c5bf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{5\\,000}{1+49{e}^{-500t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527500} {:answer/id #uuid "d5a2f479-09b1-51f2-87cb-2b2921667209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{1\\,000}{1+5{e}^{-500t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527740}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527500} :answer-field/id #uuid "93650186-de97-555d-ac5a-338706314d50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ea94a739-28fe-50a1-b5d4-4f34307441cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-83878" :question/problem "The rate of growth in the frog population $P(t)$ in a certain lake follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.1P(5\\,000-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years. Find an expression for the frog population after $t$ years, given that there were $100$ frogs initially." :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.1,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=5\\,000.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{5\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-(5\\,000\\cdot 0.1)t}}=\\frac{5\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-500t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=100$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-500\\cdot 0}} & =100 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{5\\,000}{100} \\\\ C & =50-1 \\\\  & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{5\\,000}{1+49{e}^{-500t}}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "797a3a82-2ac7-5e67-8bc3-05f3eb5bdc68"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528037 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101458 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a54e664a-f1d7-5bcb-8e94-1a6ce5022d23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The series diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099910} {:answer/id #uuid "100c1476-f281-54e0-95a7-7818ad4fe29c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099911} {:answer/id #uuid "12064d86-564b-5b56-8f66-38d0279ac64d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099912} {:answer/id #uuid "35745ab3-9c05-512d-aeca-22c906dffd62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099913} {:answer/id #uuid "43bdaffc-0049-5e1e-bbe6-abbbc04ef425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099914}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186099914} :answer-field/id #uuid "fac9b251-da24-5c85-a8c5-19a64644ffc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a76f098c-1049-59cd-81d6-41ec2e42a5cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-49685" :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+5n+6}.$\n\n*Hint: Try to factor the denominator first.*" :question/worked-solution "First, factoring the denominator and using partial fractions, we can write the series as\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+5n+6}=\\frac{1}{(n+2)(n+3)}=\\frac{1}{n+2}-\\frac{1}{n+3}.\n$$\n\nSo, our series can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n+2}-\\frac{1}{n+3}).\n$$\n\nLet's now compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1} & =(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\ {s}_{2} & =(\\frac{1}{3}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\ {s}_{3} & =(\\frac{1}{3}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\ {s}_{4} & =(\\frac{1}{3}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\frac{1}{7})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for each partial sum, we always end up canceling all terms except for the first and last terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n{s}_{N}=\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{N+3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+2)(n+3)} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{N+3}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8eb95567-cedc-5ed3-904e-251e8cbbb1f0"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528038 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527917 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40e2a484-90cb-5f0c-a2b4-636fe404086d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527434} {:answer/id #uuid "7dcbe35b-5636-570d-b3cd-c1bd82df5118" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527546} {:answer/id #uuid "81c497bd-16b3-5f8b-936f-2a23c8ee0a74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527561} {:answer/id #uuid "93c9e3b4-f68a-59e5-a47b-bd4f8b4df77a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527600} {:answer/id #uuid "eff134e1-d3ca-57b9-9c1d-9c3f3d2535e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2x+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527774}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527434} :answer-field/id #uuid "b589ba1a-614d-56c2-9925-d5bfb8d755e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0e54ebb5-b13c-5b43-a434-8a77b6f9b884" :question/math-academy-id "q-75282" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent line to the polar curve $r=1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ at $\\theta =\\pi ,$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the tangent line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-2\\cdot 0}{-0-2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(-1,0).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the tangent:\n\nWe find the equation of the tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (x-(-1)) \\\\ y-0 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (x+1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/70/7065086643b98b95b5d6face85544f33b00f73614a855fb9846ba68e5d987a33.png)"} :step/id #uuid "969393d9-e073-55ff-b0f0-afacbb8b2211"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528039 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399384 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae1e039d-033d-5fb2-afe2-058a41532346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$R=5$ and $I=[-1,1]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399386} {:answer/id #uuid "b5518e7e-ad19-5fdf-b048-f6f07aa85005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$R=1$ and $I=(-6,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399387} {:answer/id #uuid "a777899f-4fb2-599e-8bac-fd1080f27455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$R=1$ and $I=[-6,-4]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399388} {:answer/id #uuid "730ca820-682a-52b2-87ed-4d379934e772" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$R=5$ and $I=(-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399389} {:answer/id #uuid "89a3a902-db89-527e-ac98-aebc7e8b5c3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$R=1$ and $I=[-6,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399390}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399388} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3aed52a-be07-5913-ae72-4601a0fc9425" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1c3c2591-89b8-5e94-a7b0-e02f5b424693" :question/math-academy-id "q-103125" :question/problem "Find the radius of convergence $R$ and interval of convergence $I$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)}\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(x+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x+5)^{n+1}}{(n+1)(n+2)}\\cdot \\frac{n(n+1)}{(x+5)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{n(x+5)}{n+2}| \\\\  & =|x+5|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{n+2} \\\\  & =|x+5|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{(1+\\frac{2}{n})}) \\\\  & =|x+5|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|x+5|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x+5| & <1 \\\\ -1<x+5 & <1 \\\\ -6<x & <-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the radius of convergence is $R=1,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-6,-4).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x+5|=1,$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-6$ and $x=-4$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=-6$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-6+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}+n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.\n\n- When $x=-4$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-4+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{1}^{n}}{{n}^{2}+n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the above series with  ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}},$\nwe see that the series is convergent by the comparison test.\n\nSo the interval of convergence is $I=[-6,-4].$"} :step/id #uuid "99855eb4-1a2f-577f-9380-cf59936c9dff"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528040 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527956 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1424cef7-112d-5fb2-a1e4-a43f08ef3ec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r(t)=⟨2{e}^{2t},\\,3{e}^{3t}+{t}^{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527349} {:answer/id #uuid "18cfd146-d9b1-5fc7-8a5a-7d89b8a8d28d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r(t)=⟨{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{3t}+t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527359} {:answer/id #uuid "42a2d5bf-263e-583b-b1ce-d84d778a3c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r(t)=⟨{e}^{2t},\\,{e}^{3t}+t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527435} {:answer/id #uuid "86b5677b-1c5e-54d1-88ed-d310ea17fadc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r(t)=⟨4{e}^{2t}+t,\\,9{e}^{3t}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527569} {:answer/id #uuid "dde445ba-ab44-5a01-ae5f-f82976b094fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r(t)=⟨2{e}^{2t}+t,\\,{e}^{3t}+t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527747}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527435} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c828a93-5ad8-5389-8e1d-dff274396206" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "743918b2-4124-5be2-a1fd-716500b8cb35" :question/math-academy-id "q-83470" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨4{e}^{2t},\\,9{e}^{3t}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(1,1)$ and has velocity $⟨2,4⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨4{e}^{2t},\\,9{e}^{3t}⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 4{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 9{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{2t}+{C}_{1},\\,3{e}^{3t}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(0)=⟨2,4⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨2{e}^{2(0)}+{C}_{1},\\,3{e}^{3(0)}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,4⟩ & =⟨2+{C}_{1},3+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=1.$ Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨2{e}^{2t},\\,3{e}^{3t}+1⟩.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2{e}^{2t},\\,3{e}^{3t}+1⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 2{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (3{e}^{3t}+1)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{2t}+{C}_{3},\\,{e}^{3t}+t+{C}_{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨1,1⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨{e}^{2(0)}+{C}_{3},\\,{e}^{3(0)}+0+{C}_{4}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{3},\\,1+{C}_{4}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{3}={C}_{4}=0.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{e}^{2t},\\,{e}^{3t}+t⟩.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9e830132-16cf-5d39-b4af-edcf784ec17c"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528042 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527967 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0698e6b3-8baf-5fb0-aa2a-7fc2dc6f1d50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "({e}^{3}-e)\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527320} {:answer/id #uuid "3fa7013a-d3ff-5ede-95fd-816455406c82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4({e}^{3}-e)\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527431} {:answer/id #uuid "51c95b82-1f8c-5ba4-b17a-3dfc2ef2f170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{3}-e)\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527463} {:answer/id #uuid "7dae70a2-67bf-5104-822e-1f7ffe60190e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{6}-{e}^{2})\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527544} {:answer/id #uuid "e1aa870a-ac3e-585f-bd93-cba436328789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "({e}^{6}-{e}^{2})\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527753}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527320} :answer-field/id #uuid "8eb043c2-db7d-5e14-932d-20ffb968ecb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "881af2f1-aaf5-5e5d-a46a-df43ce3ea5ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-84409" :question/problem "Kevin is moving his car in a straight line. His position relative to a fixed origin $O$ is given by the function $x(t)=2{e}^{t/2}\\,\\text{m},$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. Find its average position between $2$ and $6$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the average value of the position on $[{t}_{1},{t}_{2}],$ we use the average value formula\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{{t}_{2}-{t}_{1}}\\,{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{6-2}\\,{\\int }_{2}^{6}2{e}^{t/2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {2\\cdot 2({e}^{t/2})|}_{2}^{6} \\\\  & ={e}^{6/2}-{e}^{2/2} \\\\  & ={e}^{3}-e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average position is $({e}^{3}-e)\\,\\text{m}.$"} :step/id #uuid "bf1cf1cc-9e0a-54e5-b0bd-522e2d64cd52"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528043 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527920 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0adf5f03-f503-5641-88e2-3fce055cb3d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{1}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527328} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed1b7ea-f020-53de-ba57-6c7fa5462de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527551} {:answer/id #uuid "94817e0a-ff61-5937-8f82-4fdc78a55f96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527603} {:answer/id #uuid "aa41cea0-7e1a-565b-94bf-91ca37c53552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527658} {:answer/id #uuid "f2239c74-dc79-5d0e-a6f7-211e1208eaa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527778}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527603} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dee6e17-9565-5642-81b9-7791c50bb056" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "15fdecd3-2bb4-5c94-a4a3-305e0c26984d" :question/math-academy-id "q-103902" :question/problem "Let $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and let ${P}_{3}(x)$ be the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=1.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error when ${P}_{3}(x)$ is used to approximate $\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$" :question/worked-solution "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{3}$ obtained when using the third-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{3}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{M}{4!}|x-1{|}^{4},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=1$ and $x=2.$ \n\nPlugging $x=2$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{4!}|2-1{|}^{4} \\\\ |{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{24}|1{|}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[1,2].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ |{f}^{(4)}(x)| & =|-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}|=\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|=\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}$ is a decreasing function in the interval $[1,2],$  its value is greatest at $x=1.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(4)}(1)|=\\frac{6}{(1)^{4}}=6,\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{6}{24}|1{|}^{4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bf9db98f-b58f-5afd-b4e7-7c89477ea4c3"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528044 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256380 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb529d81-61f3-5c3a-9ee9-1db6044fbb28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461965} {:answer/id #uuid "7d60155f-814b-5688-b80c-ff69952ec3fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461966}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461965} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4d0f6ff-d5d7-5577-b526-af22d3855f19" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462736} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6edd1c4-8271-5dbf-a1a6-4d911168312f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461967} {:answer/id #uuid "b0d84be9-03de-5a03-987f-f1a67f85e17f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461968}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461967} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a3087f9-0cbe-58bf-bce0-511b72140cf0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462737} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1637f773-701a-5c85-a0b6-4ffc31761b76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461969} {:answer/id #uuid "490949a0-6a54-5bc2-b39c-42807015af84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461970}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461970} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9d67781-1cc9-577e-b688-09b5d2ad50e6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "69c58940-535c-5bff-aff2-b382e99c5568" :question/math-academy-id "q-120282" :question/problem "![](images/e9/e90d9a160db2c75091253eaa8a8c815e92c45f988841683f5a4d18ca6a6ea7db.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-x{y}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{y}{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{y}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "![](images/54/54932bcffbbe793d6e1f730e65570b19cfc3603ddb62eaf0a63ef2e5b0705979.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- For $y\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $x=0$ only.\n\n- For $x\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=-x{y}^{2}$ evaluates to zero both on $x=0$ and $y=0.$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{y}{x}$ does not evaluate to zero on $x=0.$\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. For $y\\ne 0,$ the right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{y}$ evaluates to zero on $x=0$ only, and for $x\\ne 0,$ we have \n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|\\frac{x}{y}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "cf763070-22bc-59f4-804a-e6a5cae8fe8f"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528045 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341961 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48658c86-5fff-5d1e-ad97-ca9615485d8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339773} {:answer/id #uuid "8d8c23eb-6ff2-5bd1-ab4a-f009409818c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x+9\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340399} {:answer/id #uuid "a839708e-6d86-5a57-b693-6f0e21e650eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340655} {:answer/id #uuid "ced86a89-239e-50b2-894e-628af504a0d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340966} {:answer/id #uuid "daa4acb0-2d88-582f-a718-be7e56ba1265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341071}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341071} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea2bfd72-4857-53aa-b6a7-f5d94cbdaa7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "40d2e0b8-4256-5b72-ae20-79f2c7d72c06" :question/math-academy-id "q-73391" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{x-1}{3x-2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we pull out the factor of $3$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-1}{3x-2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{x-1}{3(x-\\frac{2}{3})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{x-1}{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x & {x}^{0} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 1 & -1 \\\\  & ↓ & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-1}{(x-\\frac{2}{3})} & =1+\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{3})}{(x-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{3(x-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{3x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-1}{3x-2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int (1-\\frac{1}{3x-2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\int \\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{3x-2}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[x-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|]+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d14a89a4-619d-505a-8391-cb54838fdafe"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528047 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233443 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0afd21a9-c1f9-5631-b042-8d95195cced4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$L=\\infty .$ The series is divergent." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527329} {:answer/id #uuid "0ce64f0d-e9ba-5dbb-9ffc-282418bc0004" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$L=3.$ The series is divergent." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527333} {:answer/id #uuid "106587e7-d829-54ab-8b7e-609c77cf0a14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$L=1.$ The series is divergent." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527340} {:answer/id #uuid "1b339991-d53a-533c-8056-20f5d713d32d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$L=0.$The series is convergent." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527365} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba6070b-da1a-5e0b-a72d-fc13af7e3165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$L=1.$ The root test gives no conclusion." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527584}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527584} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef334055-d235-506e-a901-ee66a80f4e9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "16eb9f4b-4d3a-531a-a851-09494af3ba39" :question/math-academy-id "q-138998" :question/problem "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{3{n}^{n}}{(1+n)^{n}}.$ According to the root test, does ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ converge or diverge?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{3{n}^{n}}{(1+n)^{n}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{3{n}^{n}}{(1+n)^{n}})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt[n]{3}\\,n}{1+n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt[n]{3}\\,n}{n(\\frac{1}{n}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt[n]{3}}{(\\frac{1}{n}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0+1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{3}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=1,$ the root test gives no conclusion."} :step/id #uuid "d9129969-af7a-5955-b7e8-6985fbf78f94"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528048 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a76db44-e626-54e3-bb32-fdade55a01be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527402} {:answer/id #uuid "35321656-4bd6-5396-805d-37ad3b680477" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527416} {:answer/id #uuid "719b0fe5-3c99-5cac-84f5-1fc289687dce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527521} {:answer/id #uuid "c50c3670-73c7-5cc3-9ccf-673bb3533570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527701} {:answer/id #uuid "c52c231c-1dfb-55b4-9960-d0a4177ddef9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527702}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527402} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb85d00d-c4fb-5b35-911e-a717db9a9969" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "dc80cc6c-733b-50ec-85a0-e9f5b6f29bef" :question/math-academy-id "q-73332" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={x}^{2}-1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,x\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $0$ | $3$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3})|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & ={\\frac{{u}^{3/2}}{3}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{3/2}}{3}-\\frac{{0}^{3/2}}{3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e0ed7a4b-b4cc-5018-b3dd-1174082a5fce"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528049 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527970 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "130ca9ad-058c-5822-8dbf-2f9979837568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(4-4{x}^{2}+{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527348} {:answer/id #uuid "1ded8cec-6c65-5b45-902e-409cb9650433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(8-4x+2{x}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527375} {:answer/id #uuid "42da8871-cac3-553a-8b22-b39bd40cd1cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(4-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527436} {:answer/id #uuid "62786fe1-c6f9-56dd-98a5-068099524de1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(8-8{x}^{2}+2{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527492} {:answer/id #uuid "9a613018-d415-54e6-ad9f-5b9ca3b555a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(2-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527624}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527492} :answer-field/id #uuid "aeff073f-a16e-5f3f-a31e-6729474c2b63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8deca7b4-0aaf-504b-958b-1e6e90c74a93" :question/math-academy-id "q-79877" :question/problem "![](images/49/49b119b378ac4f45c0c395ca77c5cefead3a7c811295b51ac8eaa8aef7100e25.png)\n\nThe base of a solid is the region enclosed by the curves $y=1-{x}^{2}$ and $y=-1,$  as shown in the picture. Cross-sections perpendicular to the $x$-axis are rectangles. If the height of each rectangle is $2$ times the length of its base, which integral gives the volume of the solid?" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=1-{x}^{2}$ and $g(x)=-1.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the two curves by setting the functions equal to one another and solving for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 1-{x}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limit of integration are $x=-\\sqrt{2}$ and $x=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nNow, consider a cross-section at some point $x$, where $x\\in (-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$,  and its projection on the $xy$-plane: \n\n![](images/0c/0c2a676ecc781a2a7a78870eb93ee9fbe5a89a7bd754a446231c6e3237f75831.png)\n\nThe base of the cross-section is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =|f(x)-g(x)| \\\\  & =|1-{x}^{2}-(-1)| \\\\  & =|2-{x}^{2}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of a rectangle is\n$A=bh.$\n  So, since we have $h=2b,$ the expression for the area of the cross-section is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(x) & =bh \\\\  & =2{b}^{2} \\\\  & =2{(2-{x}^{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =8-8{x}^{2}+2{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now calculate the volume of the solid, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}A(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(8-8{x}^{2}+2{x}^{4})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ea14f9fa-c275-53b2-b89a-fdd6643671ab"}] :activity/title "Calculus II: Placement Exam" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045462 :db/ident :activity.type/diagnostic} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527307}]}
